  "id": "fwi-rp2",
  "title": "Wildfire hazard: FWI, 2-year return period",
  "description": "The yearly maximum Fire Weather Index (FWI) at noon local time, expected once in 2 years, on a global 0.5° grid. Higher values mean weather that favors severe fire.\n\n[CSIRO](https://www.csiro.au/) computed the grid for the [ThinkHazard!](https://thinkhazard.org/) wildfire layer from the daily FWI in NASA's [Global Fire Weather Database](https://data.giss.nasa.gov/impacts/gfwed/), 1981 to 2016. The [technical basis](https://datacatalogfiles.worldbank.org/ddh-published/0066912/DR0095597/THhazardclass_Wildfire_CSIRO.pdf) gives the method and the thresholds that ThinkHazard! uses. Sibling collections hold the 10 and 30-year return periods.\n\nThis collection mirrors the [World Bank Data Catalog dataset](https://datacatalog.worldbank.org/search/dataset/0066912/global-wildfire-hazard) as a cloud-optimized GeoTIFF. Cells that GFWED masks, such as oceans and deserts, hold 0. Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) for the grid, the quirks, and recipes that run.",
